  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "Individual road-level volume errors remain as large as simple baseline predictors, and out-of-calibration sensors were still partially used to inform preliminary modeling decisions.",
      "what": "Researchers built an agent-based regional traffic simulator for Emilia-Romagna, Italy, combining mobile phone records for trip demand, loop detectors for volume calibration, and GPS traces for route choices. Evaluated on out-of-calibration sensors, the model tracked total regional traffic volume within 1% in winter 2020 and 10% in summer 2021. Integrating GPS traces improved relative road traffic ranking performance to a Spearman correlation of 0.67–0.68, outperforming static road-type predictors (0.41–0.48).",
      "why": "It isolates the specific, additive value of distinct spatial data streams in regional transport models, demonstrating that GPS improves route choice rankings while mobile phone data primarily governs temporal demand patterns."
    }
  },
